# Copy to `.npmrc` in *your* project if `npm install` reports that the sqlite3
# install script was blocked.
#
# When you need this
# ------------------
# `anydb-mcp` depends on `sqlite3`, which ships its prebuilt native binding
# through an install script. npm 12 and later block install scripts by default.
# If `db_query` answers "SQLite support is unavailable", npm blocked it.
#
# Two ways to approve it
# ----------------------
# 1. `npm install-scripts approve sqlite3`
#    This writes an `allowScripts` field into your `package.json`. It is the
#    documented default because it survives a reinstall of npm and needs no dotfile.
#
#    Note the precedence rule: a `package.json` `allowScripts` field *suppresses*
#    the `.npmrc` `allow-scripts` setting below. If you use both, only the
#    `package.json` one is read, and npm prints a warning saying so.
#
# 2. This file, copied to `.npmrc` in your project root:
#
#       allow-scripts=sqlite3
#
#    Works as long as your `package.json` has no `allowScripts` field of its own.
#
# The other four databases -- PostgreSQL, MySQL, MongoDB, Redis -- are pure
# JavaScript and are unaffected either way.
#
# Then:  npm rebuild sqlite3
